<b>Example 1:</b> The following Apache Axis 2 Rampart client configuration does not require inbound messages to be encrypted since the <code>&lt;items&gt;</code> tag does not contain an <code>Encrypt</code> directive:
<pre>
&lt;service&gt;
...
	&lt;parameter name=&quot;OutflowSecurity&quot;&gt;
		&lt;action&gt;
			&lt;items&gt;Timestamp Signature&lt;/items&gt;
			...
		&lt;/action&gt;
	&lt;/parameter&gt;
&lt;/service&gt;
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Provided the service provider supports it, configure your client to only send encrypted messages. You can do this by adding an <code>Encrypt</code> directive the <code>&lt;items&gt;</code> tag.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following shows the use of the <code>Encrypt</code> directive, which effectively fixes <code>Example 1</code>
<pre>
&lt;service&gt;
...
	&lt;parameter name=&quot;OutflowSecurity&quot;&gt;
		&lt;action&gt;
			&lt;items&gt;Timestamp Signature Encrypt&lt;/items&gt;
			...
		&lt;/action&gt;
	&lt;/parameter&gt;
&lt;/service&gt;
</pre>

Order is important! Placing the <code>Encrypt</code> directive at the end ensures that anything preceding it gets encrypted. In <code>Example 2</code>, both the timestamp and signatures will be encrypted.

Configure your service to only accept encrypted messages. You can do this by enabling confidentiality in the Request Consumer Service configuration.

The following WebSphere configuration is an example where inbound requests are expected to be encrypted:
<pre>
&lt;com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext:WsExtension xmi:version=&quot;2.0&quot; xmlns:xmi=&quot;http://www.omg.org/XMI&quot; xmlns:com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext=&quot;http://www.ibm.com/websphere/appserver/schemas/5.0.2/wsext.xmi&quot; xmi:id=&quot;WsExtension_1152150731239&quot;&gt;
...
        &lt;securityRequestConsumerServiceConfig xmi:id=&quot;SecurityRequestConsumerServiceConfig_1211399165585&quot;&gt;
          &lt;requiredConfidentiality xmi:id=&quot;RequiredConfidentiality_1211571304359&quot; name=&quot;xenc&quot; usage=&quot;Required&quot;&gt;
...
&lt;/com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext:WsExtension&gt;
</pre>

Configure your service to encrypt outgoing messages. You can do this by enabling confidentiality in the Response Generator Service configuration.

The following WebSphere configuration is an example where outbound responses are encrypted:
<pre>
&lt;com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext:WsExtension xmi:version=&quot;2.0&quot; xmlns:xmi=&quot;http://www.omg.org/XMI&quot; xmlns:com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext=&quot;http://www.ibm.com/websphere/appserver/schemas/5.0.2/wsext.xmi&quot; xmi:id=&quot;WsExtension_1152150731239&quot;&gt;
        ...
        &lt;securityResponseGeneratorServiceConfig xmi:id=&quot;SecurityResponseGeneratorServiceConfig_1211568020342&quot;&gt;
          &lt;confidentiality xmi:id=&quot;Confidentiality_1211571304359&quot; name=&quot;xenc&quot; order=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
          ...
&lt;/com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext:WsExtension&gt;
</pre>

Provided the service provider supports it, configure your client to accept and decrypt incoming messages. You can do this by enabling confidentiality in the Response Consumer Service configuration.

The following WebSphere configuration is an example where inbound messages are expected to be encrypted:
<pre>
&lt;com.ibm.etools.webservice.wscext:WsClientExtension xmi:version=&quot;2.0&quot; xmlns:xmi=&quot;http://www.omg.org/XMI&quot; xmlns:com.ibm.etools.webservice.wscext=&quot;http://www.ibm.com/websphere/appserver/schemas/5.0.2/wscext.xmi&quot; xmi:id=&quot;WsClientExtension_1152150778436&quot;&gt;
        ...
