          ...
&lt;/com.ibm.etools.webservice.wsext:WsExtension&gt;
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Applications that transmit messages without transport or message security cannot guarantee the integrity or confidentiality of the messages. When a WCF security binding is set to <code>None</code>, both transport and message security are disabled.

<Paragraph>
In this case, the security mode is set to <code>None</code> in <Replace key="PrimaryLocation.file"/> on line <Replace key="PrimaryLocation.line"/>.
</Paragraph>

<b>Example 1:</b> The following configuration sets the security mode to <code>None</code>.

<pre>
&lt;system.serviceModel&gt;
&lt;bindings&gt;
  &lt;wsHttpBinding&gt;
    &lt;binding name=&quot;MyBinding&quot;&gt;
      &lt;security mode=&quot;None&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/binding&gt;
&lt;/bindings&gt;
&lt;/system.serviceModel&gt;
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Use transport and message security to protect the integrity and confidentiality of messages.
<b>Example 2:</b> The following configuration tells the service to use transport security with message credentials.

<pre>
&lt;system.serviceModel&gt;
&lt;bindings&gt;
  &lt;wsHttpBinding&gt;
    &lt;binding name=&quot;MyBinding&quot;&gt;
      &lt;security mode=&quot;TransportWithMessageCredential&quot;/&gt;
      &lt;message clientCredentialType=&quot;Windows&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/binding&gt;
&lt;/bindings&gt;
&lt;/system.serviceModel&gt;
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Service Providers that use the UsernameToken might accept passwords sent in plain text. Sending plain text passwords over an unencrypted channel can expose the credential to any attacker who can sniff the SOAP message.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Axis 2 service provider configuration uses a <code>UsernameToken</code>:
<pre>
&lt;service&gt;
...
    &lt;parameter name=&quot;InflowSecurity&quot;&gt;
      &lt;action&gt;
        &lt;items&gt;UsernameToken&lt;/items&gt;
...
&lt;/service&gt;
</pre>

Description.Recommendations:
Require a signature instead of using the <code>UsernameToken</code>.

<b>Example 2:</b> The following Axis 2 service provider configuration uses an encrypted signature instead of <code>UsernameToken</code>.
<pre>
&lt;service&gt;
...
    &lt;parameter name=&quot;InflowSecurity&quot;&gt;
      &lt;action&gt;
        &lt;items&gt;Timestamp Signature Encrypt&lt;/items&gt;
...
&lt;/service&gt;
</pre>

Description.Explanation:
Sending plain text passwords over an unencrypted channel can expose the credential to attackers who can sniff the SOAP message.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following Axis 2 client configuration uses <code>UsernameToken</code> (a password):
