Use a password type of <code>PasswordDigest</code> and ensure that credentials are encrypted when sent. Sending SOAP messages over HTTPS will ensure credentials are protected with strong encryption, but if messages could be relayed using multiple transports, a better option is to encrypt the message itself using WS-Security.

The following shows a client configuration where the password type is set to <code>PasswordDigest</code>.
<pre>
&lt;axisconfig name=&quot;AxisJava2.0&quot;&gt;
...
    &lt;parameter name=&quot;OutflowSecurity&quot;&gt;
      &lt;action&gt;
        &lt;items&gt;UsernameToken&lt;/items&gt;
...
        &lt;passwordType&gt;PasswordDigest&lt;/passwordType&gt;
...
&lt;/axisconfig&gt;
</pre>

Use a password type of <code>PasswordDigest</code> and ensure that credentials are encrypted when sent. Sending SOAP messages over HTTPS will ensure credentials are protected with strong encryption, but if messages could be relayed using multiple transports, a better option is to encrypt the message itself using WS-Security.

The following shows a client configuration where the password type is set to <code>PasswordDigest</code>.
<pre>
&lt;deployment xmlns=&quot;http://xml.apache.org/axis/wsdd/&quot; xmlns:java=&quot;http://xml.apache.org/axis/wsdd/providers/java&quot;&gt;
...
  &lt;requestFlow&gt;
...
	&lt;parameter name=&quot;passwordType&quot; value=&quot;PasswordDigest&quot; /&gt;
...
&lt;/deployment&gt;
</pre>

Use a password type of <code>PasswordDigest</code> and ensure that credentials are encrypted when sent. Sending SOAP messages over HTTPS will ensure credentials are protected with strong encryption, but if messages might be relayed over multiple transports, a better option is to encrypt the message itself using WS-Security.

The following shows a service provider configuration where the password type is set to <code>PasswordDigest</code>.
<pre>
&lt;deployment xmlns=&quot;http://xml.apache.org/axis/wsdd/&quot; xmlns:java=&quot;http://xml.apache.org/axis/wsdd/providers/java&quot;&gt;
...
